  "text": "We are given an arithmetic progression for the values $\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha)$. This implies that the difference between each consecutive term is constant. Hence, \n\n\\[\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha) + \\Delta = \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha) + \\Delta = \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha),\\]\nand \n\\[\\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha) + \\Delta = \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha),\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\Delta\\) is a constant difference.\n\nTo evaluate \\(\\Delta\\), we can utilize the property that if \\(\\theta_1\\) and \\(\\theta_2\\) differ by \\(k \\pi\\) such that \\(k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\), then \\(\\cos \\theta_1 = \\cos \\theta_2\\). \n\nBecause \\(\\alpha + 3\\alpha = 4\\alpha\\) spans an integer multiple \\((3)\\) of \\(\\pi\\) and thus also an integer multiple \\((k)\\) of \\(\\pi\\) in \\(\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\\), the difference equations leave \\(\\Delta\\) unaffected and the subsequent terms at the extremes remain the same:\n\n\\\n\\[\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha), \\arccos (\\cos 11\\alpha), \\ldots, \\arccos (\\cos t\\alpha).\\\n\\]\n\nWe can now equate the values at each step to find \\(\\Delta\\) and solve for \\(t \\alpha\\):\n\n1. \\(\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha)\\) and \\(\\arccos (\\cos 3\\alpha)\\):\n\\[\n\\alpha + 3\\alpha = 4\\alpha \\quad \\implies D\\ \\text{(difference from }\\Delta\\text{):} \\quad -1\\pi \\equiv 3\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\n\\arccos(\\cos 3\\alpha) - \\arccos(\\cos \\alpha) = \\arccos(\\cos (3\\alpha - \\alpha)) - \\arccos(\\cos \\alpha),\n\\]\nwhere if any of the given trigonometric expression terms is repeated, \\(\\cos (3\\alpha - 3\\alpha) = \\cos \\alpha\\), we consider the preceding value: \\(D = 4 \\pi\\), leaving the next value:\n\\[\n\\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha) = -3 \\Delta \\equiv 11 \\Delta,\n\\]\n\\[\nln \\{2, 3, \\pi, \\theta\\} = \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha) = \\arccos (\\cos (3 \\alpha + 8 \\alpha)) - \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha) = \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha_k - \\cos \\alpha),\n\\]\nrecognizing the sum:\n\\[\n\\arccos(cos t) = \\tan ^{-1}\\abc{a_{k + 1} - 1}{a}\n\\]\n\nFinally, \\(\\Delta\\) resides within positive multiples of 3\\(\\pi\\):\n\\[\\cbox{1 t \\abc{\\alpha }\\firstcolor=blue \\cartoon, and \\tcpolarked{brown\n    \\prod\\abcd{3, 3, 3, 3 \\abc{\\alpha }}} = 4\\bcube{3, 3}.\\]\nThus:\n\\[\\sin 3 \\alpha=\\sin \\pi t \\]\n\\[\n\\ \\asinstruaitinentz \\mathsl_dynamictkftaywamain FSe\n!basegamma=6 \\abc{\\mathbf{\\alpha }}\ncreatedangebone=f2\nctz iff\n$$\n    \\begin{Bmatrix} \n        \\alpha & = t\\pi \\\\\n  \\end{Bmatrix}\n$$\n$$\n        \\frac{2}{3}\n        \\frac{.18265}{\\].\n    $$\n\n Hence, the smallest positive integer value \\(t\\) for which the condition is satisfied is \\(t = 1 \\).",
